markdown <<MARKDOWN
# Prompting for Loops 

Now that students are working with loops and collections of data, prompting becomes even more important.

In Unit 4, students are no longer only describing what an app should display. They also need to explain how the program should repeat logic across arrays and datasets.

---

## Strong Prompt Checklist

<div style="display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(2, 1fr);gap:15px;width:100%;margin:20px 0px;">

  <div style="background-color:#F5F7F8;color:#000000;padding:20px;border-radius:10px;box-shadow:2px 2px 8px rgba(0,0,0,0.1);">
    <p style="font-size:12pt;font-weight:bold;margin:0;">🔁 Did I explain the loop behavior?</p>
    <p style="font-size:12pt;line-height:1.3;margin-top:10px;">
      Did I describe what should happen for each item in the array or dataset?
    </p>
  </div>

  <div style="background-color:#F5F7F8;color:#000000;padding:20px;border-radius:10px;box-shadow:2px 2px 8px rgba(0,0,0,0.1);">
    <p style="font-size:12pt;font-weight:bold;margin:0;">🧠 Did I include context?</p>
    <p style="font-size:12pt;line-height:1.3;margin-top:10px;">
      Did I explain what arrays, objects, buttons, or variables already exist?
    </p>
  </div>

  <div style="background-color:#F5F7F8;color:#000000;padding:20px;border-radius:10px;box-shadow:2px 2px 8px rgba(0,0,0,0.1);">
    <p style="font-size:12pt;font-weight:bold;margin:0;">⚙️ Did I include conditions?</p>
    <p style="font-size:12pt;line-height:1.3;margin-top:10px;">
      Did I explain what the program should check for while looping through the data?
    </p>
  </div>

  <div style="background-color:#F5F7F8;color:#000000;padding:20px;border-radius:10px;box-shadow:2px 2px 8px rgba(0,0,0,0.1);">
    <p style="font-size:12pt;font-weight:bold;margin:0;">👀 Did I describe the result?</p>
    <p style="font-size:12pt;line-height:1.3;margin-top:10px;">
      Did I explain what the user should see after the loop finishes running?
    </p>
  </div>

</div>

---

<div style="background-color:#e4e2f8;border-radius:30px;color:#000000;width:95%;padding:18px 24px;margin:24px 0;">

<p style="font-size:16px;line-height:1.5;margin:0;">
<strong>💡 Teacher Tip:</strong> Students often describe what they want the app to display, but forget to explain how the program should process the data. Encourage students to describe the loop behavior, the condition being checked, and what should happen for each item in the collection.
</p>

</div>

## Try It: Revise the Prompt

**A student writes this prompt**:

<div style="background-color:#F5F7F8;border-radius:14px;padding:18px 22px;margin:20px 0;box-shadow:2px 2px 8px rgba(0,0,0,0.08);">
  <p style="font-size:12pt;line-height:1.5;margin:0;">
    “Make my music app show only songs by the artist the user picks.”
  </p>
</div>

This prompt has a clear goal, but it may not give the AI enough information to create useful code.

Before expanding the sections below, think about how you would coach a student to make this prompt stronger.

<details style="background-color:#ececec;padding:12px;margin-bottom:10px;border:1px solid transparent;border-radius:8px;">
  <summary style="font-size:16px;font-weight:bold;">🔁 What loop behavior should the student describe?</summary>
  <div style="margin-top:16px;margin-left:16px;margin-right:16px;">
    <p style="font-size:16px;line-height:1.5;">
      The program needs to loop through each song in the collection and check one song at a time.
    </p>
  </div>
</details>

<details style="background-color:#ececec;padding:12px;margin-bottom:10px;border:1px solid transparent;border-radius:8px;">
  <summary style="font-size:16px;font-weight:bold;">🧠 What data context should the student include?</summary>
  <div style="margin-top:16px;margin-left:16px;margin-right:16px;">
    <p style="font-size:16px;line-height:1.5;">
      They should include the data structure, such as an array of song objects, and describe which property stores the artist name.
    </p>
  </div>
</details>

<details style="background-color:#ececec;padding:12px;margin-bottom:10px;border:1px solid transparent;border-radius:8px;">
  <summary style="font-size:16px;font-weight:bold;">⚙️ What condition should the student clarify?</summary>
  <div style="margin-top:16px;margin-left:16px;margin-right:16px;">
    <p style="font-size:16px;line-height:1.5;">
      The program should compare each song’s artist to the artist selected by the user.
    </p>
  </div>
</details>

<details style="background-color:#ececec;padding:12px;margin-bottom:10px;border:1px solid transparent;border-radius:8px;">
  <summary style="font-size:16px;font-weight:bold;">👀 What result should the student describe?</summary>
  <div style="margin-top:16px;margin-left:16px;margin-right:16px;">
    <p style="font-size:16px;line-height:1.5;">
      The student should describe what the user should see after the loop runs, such as a list of matching song titles displayed on the page.
    </p>
  </div>
</details>



<details style="background-color:#FFFFFF;padding:12px;margin-top:20px;margin-bottom:10px;border:1px solid #D9E3E5;border-radius:8px;box-shadow:2px 2px 8px rgba(0,0,0,0.06);">
  <summary style="font-size:16px;font-weight:bold;">✅ One Possible Revision</summary>

  <div style="margin-top:16px;margin-left:16px;margin-right:16px;">

<div style="background-color:#EAF7F8;border-left:6px solid #048899;border-radius:12px;padding:16px 20px;">
      <p style="font-size:16px;line-height:1.5;margin:0;">
        “I am building a music app with an array of song objects. Each song object has a title and artist property. Write JavaScript code that loops through the array, checks whether each song’s artist matches the artist selected by the user, and displays only the matching song titles on the page. Use clear variable names and beginner-friendly comments.”
      </p>
    </div>

  </div>
</details>



MARKDOWN
